WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.065
This chapter is about chest findings that point to a mechanical problem.

2
00:00:06.065 --> 00:00:10.394
We start with tracheal shift, which way it goes and why.

3
00:00:10.394 --> 00:00:21.525
Then paradoxical breathing, where the chest or abdomen moves the wrong way, and flail chest, where a segment of the chest wall moves on its own.

4
00:00:21.525 --> 00:00:36.675
We finish with the emergency that ties them together, the tension pneumothorax: how to recognize it, what to recommend and in what order, what the film shows, and needle thoracostomy step by step.

5
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.160
The deck's memory trick is toward loss, away from pressure.

6
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:56.540
The trachea shifts toward the affected side when lung volume is lost, because the volume loss pulls it, as in atelectasis, fibrosis or a surgical resection.

7
00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:12.064
It shifts away from the affected side when something occupies space, because the pressure pushes it, as in a large or tension pneumothorax, a large pleural effusion or a large mass.

8
00:01:12.064 --> 00:01:20.983
To check it, RCP 103 says to place an index finger at the sternal notch and move it gently side to side.

9
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:25.100
Normally the trachea is midline above the notch.

10
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.359
The RCP 203 notes give three bedside patterns.

11
00:01:30.359 --> 00:01:37.407
A pneumothorax gives a trachea pushed away, hyperresonance and absent breath sounds.

12
00:01:37.407 --> 00:01:46.804
A pleural effusion gives a trachea pushed away, decreased or absent fremitus, dullness and absent breath sounds.

13
00:01:46.804 --> 00:01:53.264
Atelectasis gives a trachea pulled toward, dullness and absent breath sounds.

14
00:01:53.264 --> 00:02:07.695
The nuance: a shift with a pneumothorax generally implies a large or tension pneumothorax, not every pneumothorax, and an effusion must be substantial to shift the trachea.

15
00:02:07.695 --> 00:02:24.895
The Trauma deck agrees that deviation away in a pneumothorax is a late and unreliable finding, so do not wait for it, or for a film, before treating a suspected tension pneumothorax in an unstable patient.

16
00:02:24.895 --> 00:02:31.775
Hess adds that on a chest film, rotation alone can make the trachea look deviated.

17
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:46.651
The RCP 203 notes define paradoxical breathing as the chest wall or abdominal wall moving in on inspiration and out on exhalation, the opposite of normal.

18
00:02:46.651 --> 00:02:54.425
It is a sign of respiratory distress from damage to the structures involved in breathing.

19
00:02:54.425 --> 00:03:07.003
Together with dyspnea, tachypnea and accessory muscle use, thoraco-abdominal paradox, the see-saw, signals an abnormally high work of breathing.

20
00:03:07.003 --> 00:03:10.060
Hess tells you what each one means.

21
00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:16.873
If the abdomen moves in on inspiration, think diaphragm weakness or paralysis.

22
00:03:16.873 --> 00:03:29.800
If the chest wall moves in, think paralysis of the chest wall muscles, as in a high thoracic or low cervical spinal cord injury, or a flail segment.

23
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.255
Paradoxical breathing matters most during weaning.

24
00:03:35.255 --> 00:03:47.419
Thoraco-abdominal paradox means you should stop a spontaneous breathing trial, and paradoxical abdominal movement is an early sign of weaning failure.

25
00:03:47.419 --> 00:03:55.286
In spinal cord injury and severe neuromuscular disease, the chest moves inward during inhalation.

26
00:03:55.286 --> 00:04:07.450
And Hess chapter forty four adds that paradoxical motion of the rib cage and abdomen may indicate impending respiratory failure or diaphragm weakness.

27
00:04:08.867 --> 00:04:25.233
The RCP 202 Trauma deck defines a flail chest as three or more ribs fractured in two or more places, a sternal fracture, or a costochondral separation, creating an unstable segment of chest wall.

28
00:04:25.233 --> 00:04:29.430
Flail refers to that segment's paradoxical motion.

29
00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:38.578
On inspiration, the negative intrapleural pressure draws the segment in while the rest of the thorax expands.

30
00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:41.600
On exhalation, the opposite happens.

31
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:54.022
One conflict to know: the Trauma deck says three or more ribs, but Hess chapter forty one says two or more, and Hess chapter one just says multiple.

32
00:04:54.022 --> 00:04:56.792
Use three or more, from the deck.

33
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.129
The chain to respiratory failure starts with pain.

34
00:05:02.129 --> 00:05:09.986
Pain causes shallow breaths and a poor cough, and those lead to atelectasis and retained secretions.

35
00:05:09.986 --> 00:05:24.051
Pulmonary contusions frequently accompany a flail chest and may worsen over the first twenty four to forty eight hours, impairing gas exchange with ventilation perfusion mismatch.

36
00:05:24.051 --> 00:05:35.602
Treatment is pain management, pulmonary hygiene, oxygenation, and ventilatory support when necessary, but not every flail patient needs intubation.

37
00:05:35.602 --> 00:05:50.846
Hess adds that a contusion is almost certain once a flail segment is found, and often blossoms at forty eight hours, so elective intubation before respiratory failure should be considered early.

38
00:05:50.846 --> 00:06:01.375
Other decks list a crushed or flail chest as an indication for controlled ventilation, and as a contraindication to prone positioning.

39
00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:03.652
Pause here.

40
00:06:03.652 --> 00:06:13.337
After a car crash, part of a patient's right chest wall is drawn in on every inspiration while the rest of the chest expands.

41
00:06:13.337 --> 00:06:22.325
What is this called, how does the Trauma deck define it, and why might the patient get worse over the next two days?

42
00:06:23.233 --> 00:06:29.233
[6-second practice pause]

43
00:06:29.733 --> 00:06:31.481
This is a flail chest.

44
00:06:31.481 --> 00:06:46.658
The Trauma deck defines it as three or more ribs fractured in two or more places, a sternal fracture, or a costochondral separation, which leaves an unstable segment that moves paradoxically.

45
00:06:46.658 --> 00:06:57.942
The patient may worsen because pulmonary contusions frequently come with it and may get worse over the first twenty four to forty eight hours.

46
00:06:57.942 --> 00:07:08.033
Hess says the contusion often blossoms at forty eight hours, so consider elective intubation early, before respiratory failure.

47
00:07:09.433 --> 00:07:11.935
The deck puts it in one line.

48
00:07:11.935 --> 00:07:22.113
A simple pneumothorax mainly compromises breathing; a tension pneumothorax compromises both breathing and circulation.

49
00:07:22.113 --> 00:07:25.908
The difference is not how much lung is down.

50
00:07:25.908 --> 00:07:28.582
It is the one-way valve effect.

51
00:07:28.582 --> 00:07:36.776
Air enters the pleural space but cannot adequately escape, so the pressure rises progressively.

52
00:07:36.776 --> 00:07:47.558
The mediastinum shifts, venous return falls, cardiac output drops, and the end point is obstructive shock and cardiac arrest.

53
00:07:48.967 --> 00:08:08.830
The deck's clinical signs are sudden respiratory distress or increased work of breathing, decreased chest expansion on the affected side, decreased or absent breath sounds on that side, tracheal deviation away from it, and a hyperresonant percussion note.

54
00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:13.426
The chest film shows absent lung markings and radiolucency.

55
00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:23.318
On the ventilator, watch for a sudden rise in airway pressure in volume control, or a fall in tidal volume in pressure control.

56
00:08:23.318 --> 00:08:35.392
The NBRC pearl, printed verbatim in the deck: tracheal deviation is not required to diagnose tension pneumothorax, and it is a late and unreliable finding.

57
00:08:35.392 --> 00:08:48.167
Recognize the combination of respiratory distress, one-sided diminished breath sounds and hemodynamic instability instead of waiting for deviation, or for the film.

58
00:08:49.567 --> 00:08:56.372
First, a stat chest radiograph, and place the patient on one hundred percent oxygen.

59
00:08:56.372 --> 00:09:09.414
If the patient is on positive pressure ventilation, change the settings to minimize peak inspiratory pressure, by lowering the peak pressure or the tidal volume.

60
00:09:09.414 --> 00:09:21.404
If tension is suspected and the situation appears life threatening, recommend immediate needle decompression, or a chest tube, on the affected side.

61
00:09:21.404 --> 00:09:24.725
Why does one hundred percent oxygen help?

62
00:09:24.725 --> 00:09:28.938
It is nitrogen washout applied to the pleural space.

63
00:09:28.938 --> 00:09:31.449
Pleural gas is mostly nitrogen.

64
00:09:31.449 --> 00:09:43.925
Pure oxygen strips nitrogen out of the alveoli, lowering its partial pressure on the blood side, so the pleural air is reabsorbed faster than on room air.

65
00:09:43.925 --> 00:09:52.917
It works best for small, uncomplicated pneumothoraces, and it is no substitute for decompression under tension.

66
00:09:54.333 --> 00:09:55.630
Pause and think.

67
00:09:55.630 --> 00:10:00.167
A patient on volume control suddenly becomes distressed.

68
00:10:00.167 --> 00:10:11.347
The peak airway pressure jumps, breath sounds are absent on the right, the right side is hyperresonant, and the blood pressure is falling.

69
00:10:11.347 --> 00:10:13.697
The trachea is still midline.

70
00:10:13.697 --> 00:10:20.583
What do you suspect, does the midline trachea rule it out, and what do you recommend?

71
00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:27.500
[6-second practice pause]

72
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.918
Suspect a right tension pneumothorax.

73
00:10:30.918 --> 00:10:41.799
A midline trachea does not rule it out, because tracheal deviation is a late and unreliable finding and is not required for the diagnosis.

74
00:10:41.799 --> 00:10:55.204
The combination of distress, one-sided absent breath sounds and hyperresonance, a sudden rise in airway pressure on volume control, and hemodynamic instability is enough.

75
00:10:55.204 --> 00:11:12.000
Because the situation is life threatening, recommend immediate needle decompression or a chest tube on the affected side, put the patient on one hundred percent oxygen, and lower the peak pressure or tidal volume.

76
00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:32.564
The chapter fifteen deck lists the predisposing factors: mechanical ventilation with high airway pressures, above forty to forty five centimeters of water, chest trauma, and conditions with excessively high lung compliance such as advanced emphysema.

77
00:11:32.564 --> 00:11:41.839
Positive pressure makes it worse, because pleural pressure builds fast and compresses the heart, lungs and great vessels.

78
00:11:41.839 --> 00:11:55.561
Suspect it when one of those factors comes with a rapid decline: hypotension, hypoxemia or more work of breathing, and on volume control a rapid rise in peak and plateau pressure.

79
00:11:55.561 --> 00:12:00.314
Untreated, it leads to shock or pulseless electrical activity.

80
00:12:00.314 --> 00:12:14.725
On the affected side, the film shows hyperlucency in the pleural space with a collapsed lung, a flattened diaphragm and widened rib spaces, and the mediastinum shifts to the opposite side.

81
00:12:16.133 --> 00:12:18.499
The deck gives seven steps.

82
00:12:18.499 --> 00:12:22.180
One, sit the patient upright if tolerated.

83
00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:28.753
Two, the puncture site is the second intercostal space, midclavicular line.

84
00:12:28.753 --> 00:12:32.609
Three, prep with Betadine, alcohol, or both.

85
00:12:32.609 --> 00:12:41.109
Four, insert the angiocath over the top of the third rib until the hub is against the chest wall.

86
00:12:41.109 --> 00:12:47.244
Five, listen for a rush of air and watch the cardiorespiratory status.

87
00:12:47.244 --> 00:12:54.868
Six, remove the needle, secure the angiocath, and attach a flutter, or Heimlich, valve.

88
00:12:54.868 --> 00:12:58.724
Seven, immediately prepare for a chest tube.

89
00:12:58.724 --> 00:13:03.018
Going over the top of the rib is the deck's step.

90
00:13:03.018 --> 00:13:12.833
The reason, that the intercostal vessels and nerve run under each rib, is general anatomy, not a deck statement.

91
00:13:14.233 --> 00:13:19.050
The notes give a one-line ladder for every pneumothorax.

92
00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:22.663
Small and stable: observe and give oxygen.

93
00:13:22.663 --> 00:13:25.072
Moderate: needle aspiration.

94
00:13:25.072 --> 00:13:28.082
Large or symptomatic: a chest tube.

95
00:13:28.082 --> 00:13:33.157
Tension: emergency needle decompression, then a chest tube.

96
00:13:33.157 --> 00:13:39.953
Recurrent: surgery, either video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery or pleurodesis.

97
00:13:39.953 --> 00:13:50.877
A Heimlich valve is a one-way valve used on its own, without water seal or suction, and its arrow points away from the patient.

98
00:13:50.877 --> 00:14:00.683
The idea that the needle turns a tension pneumothorax into a simple one is general teaching, not a deck statement.

99
00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.139
Here is what to remember.

100
00:14:04.139 --> 00:14:13.108
The trachea shifts toward loss and away from pressure, and in a pneumothorax the shift is late and unreliable.

101
00:14:13.108 --> 00:14:24.769
Paradoxical breathing means the wall moves in on inspiration; abdominal paradox points to the diaphragm, and it means stop the breathing trial.

102
00:14:24.769 --> 00:14:34.472
A flail chest is three or more ribs broken in two or more places, and pain and contusion drive the respiratory failure.

103
00:14:34.472 --> 00:14:39.446
A tension pneumothorax compromises breathing and circulation.

104
00:14:39.446 --> 00:14:44.257
Do not wait for deviation or a film in an unstable patient.

105
00:14:44.257 --> 00:14:57.875
Recommend a stat film and one hundred percent oxygen, lower the pressures, and decompress at the second intercostal space, midclavicular line, then place a chest tube.
